<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tanyus Shahin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1815">1815</a>–<a href="/years/1895">1895</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Tanious Chahine</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tanyus Shahin was a Maronite muleteer and blacksmith of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keserwan">Keserwan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon">Mount Lebanon</a> who led the peasant revolt of 1858 to 1859 against the Khazen sheikhs. The rebels expelled the lords, seized their property and set up a peasant government under his leadership. The revolt alarmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> lords in the south and helped bring about the civil war of 1860.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tanyus-shahin.jpg" alt="Tanyus Shahin" width="320" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown; derivative work by MagentaGreen. Please note: Derivative works#What is a derivative work? · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tanyus_Shahin_(retouched).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Shahin was born to a poor Maronite family in the village of Rayfoun in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keserwan">Keserwan</a>. Little is known of his early life: the historian Elizabeth Thompson thinks he may have been literate, while Kamal Salibi calls him half-literate. Tall and strongly built, he acted as a shaykh shabab, a village strongman with a following of armed young men, and was known for sermon-like speeches at village meetings. European diplomats called him a ruffian, but many peasants saw him as their redeemer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Peasant anger in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keserwan">Keserwan</a> had grown under the burdens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corvee-labour">corvée</a>, debt and scarce land, as the indebted Khazen sheikhs pressed their tenants harder in the late 1850s. In October 1858 several villages formed an alliance against the Khazens, and in December it chose Shahin as its leader. In January 1859 he besieged the sheikhs at Ghosta with 800 fighters. Their estates were plundered and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and wheat shared among the peasants, and by July between 500 and 600 members of the family had fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>.</p><p>Shahin declared a jumhuriyya, a republic, governed by a council of 100 village representatives, over half of them landless peasants, and kept a militia of about 1,000 men. He justified the revolt by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-gulhane">Edict of Gülhane</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanzimat">Tanzimat</a> promise of equality, which the Ottoman authorities did not read as equality between classes. His men also raided Shia villages in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a>, and Maronite and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> notables joined in a pact at Mdayrij to restore the Khazens.</p><p>In late May 1860 Shahin led some 300 men into Antelias, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> took as a provocation. Fighting at Beit Mery from 29 May began the civil war, in which the Druze defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keserwan">Keserwan</a> fighters and burned 35 to 40 Christian villages in Matn. Shahin claimed he could raise 50,000 men but sent no help to besieged Zahle, and on 29 July he declared his obedience to the Ottoman sultan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the war Youssef Karam, the acting qaimaqam, ordered the restoration of the Khazens' property. In late March 1861 Karam's forces defeated Shahin between Rayfoun and Ashqout, and on 12 April Shahin submitted under the mediation of the French general Charles de Beaufort, giving up his republic later that year. He served as a judicial official in Rayfoun and died there in 1895 in relative obscurity, leaving no memoirs.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tanyus Shahin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1858 Maronite peasants of Kisrawan led by <b>Tanyus Shahin</b> rose against their Khazin overlords and expelled them, a social revolt that alarmed Druze lords in the south.</blockquote><a href="/state/mount-lebanon-double-qaimaqamate">Mount Lebanon Double Qaimaqamate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Rayfoun, in what is now <a href="/countries/lebanon">Lebanon</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.99,35.73&amp;year=1867" aria-label="Rayfoun on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5461 932 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Tanyus Shahin"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5911 1202h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5911 1202h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.99,35.73&amp;year=1867">What was here in 1867? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
